  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:10:1",
      "id": "prop-polynomially-related-encodings-preserve-polynomial-size",
      "location": "Proof step 1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The cited definition says only that the comparison functions are “polynomials p,q: N→N”; it does not require them to be nondecreasing. Step 1.2 infers m(x)≤p(q(|x|_tau)) from m(x)≤p(|x|_sigma) and |x|_sigma≤q(|x|_tau), which requires monotonicity of p. For a one-point instance set, take effective encodings with |sigma(x)|=0 and |tau(x)|=1, q(t)=t, m(x)=1, and p(t)=(t-1)^2. Clause 1 holds since 1≤p(0)=1, while the asserted conclusion gives 1≤p(q(1))=p(1)=0. Thus the written inference is invalid under the current definition.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "b0edf8e6e1246628446f466c7089635babd4c4b11a850df8b012af2eb03ab45c"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:10:2",
      "id": "prop-polynomially-related-encodings-preserve-polynomial-size",
      "location": "Proof step 1.4",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "This is the same unsupported monotonicity inference in the reverse direction. On a one-point instance set, take effective encodings with |sigma(x)|=1 and |tau(x)|=0, s(t)=t, m(x)=1, and r(t)=(t-1)^2. Clause 2 holds since 1≤r(0)=1, and the encodings are polynomially related (use the other comparison polynomial q(t)=1). But step 1.4 asserts 1≤r(s(1))=r(1)=0. No monotonicity condition for r appears in def-instance-size-under-an-encoding.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "b0edf8e6e1246628446f466c7089635babd4c4b11a850df8b012af2eb03ab45c"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:10:3",
      "id": "lem-kleene-star-has-the-expected-least-closure-property",
      "location": "Proof step 3.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "The concluding claim says L* is the least concatenation-closed language containing L and epsilon. Step 3.1 correctly shows that every concatenation-closed K containing L and epsilon contains L*, but the proof never establishes that L* itself is concatenation-closed: clauses 1 and 2 give epsilon∈L* and LL*⊆L*, not L*L*⊆L*. A short induction on the number of L-factors in the first word would supply the omitted qualification.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
